%[text] %[text:anchor:T_92F22C48] # Finite Element Formulation of the linear Reissner-Mindlin Plate
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D949C6AD] ## Introduction
%[text] 
%[text] In this section it is considered a shear-deformable plate that is fixed along a portion of its boundary (Dirichlet boundary conditions) whereas distributed/boundary forces/monents are applied within/along a portion of its surface/boundary, see the following figure accordingly:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![problemSetting.png](text:image:7cd5)
%[text] This kind of problem can be mathematically described using the Reissner-Mindlin plate theory. The primal uknown fields of the Reissner-Mindlin plate are the vertical deflection field $w$ and the two total cross-sectional rotation fields $\\beta\_x,\\beta\_y$ around the x- and the y-axes, respectively. The underlying Partial Differential Equation (PDE), or Boundary Value Problem (BVP), or equivalently strong formulation of the problem writes as follows:
%[text] $\\begin{array}{lll}\n\\frac{\\partial q\_x}{ \\partial x} + \\frac{\\partial q\_y}{ \\partial y} + \\bar{p} = 0, & \\text{in} \\: \\Omega, & \\text{(1.1)} \\\\\n-\\frac{\\partial m\_y}{ \\partial y} + \\frac{\\partial m\_{xy}}{ \\partial x} + q\_y = 0, & \\text{in} \\: \\Omega, & \\text{(1.2)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial m\_x}{ \\partial x} + \\frac{\\partial m\_{yx}}{ \\partial y} - q\_x = 0, & \\text{in} \\: \\Omega, & \\text{(1.3)} \\\\\nq\_x n\_x + q\_y n\_y = \\bar{P}, & \\text{on} \\: \\subset \\partial \\Omega, & \\text{(1.4)} \\\\\nm\_{xy} n\_x + m\_y n\_y = - \\bar{M\_x}, & \\text{on} \\: \\subset \\partial \\Omega, & \\text{(1.4)} \\\\\nm\_x n\_x + m\_{xy} n\_y = \\bar{M\_y}, & \\text{on} \\: \\subset \\partial \\Omega, & \\text{(1.4)} \\\\\n\\end{array}$
%[text] The corresponding strains are the shear deformations of the cross sections $\\gamma\_x,\\gamma\_y$ and the change in the curvature second-order tensor $\\kappa\_x,\\kappa\_y,\\kappa\_{xy}$ and they given by the following expressions:
%[text] $\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\gamma\_x = \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} + \\beta\_y = w\_{,x} + \\beta\_y & \\text{(2.1)} \\\\\n\\gamma\_y = \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} - \\beta\_x = w\_{,y} - \\beta\_x  & \\text{(2.2)} \\\\\n\\kappa\_x = \\frac{\\partial \\beta\_x}{\\partial y} = \\beta\_{x,y} & \\text{(2.3)} \\\\\n\\kappa\_y = \\frac{\\partial \\beta\_y}{\\partial x} = \\beta\_{y,x} & \\text{(2.4)} \\\\\n\\kappa\_{xy} =  \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{\\partial \\beta\_y}{\\partial y} - \\frac{\\partial \\beta\_x}{\\partial x} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\beta\_{y,y} - \\beta\_{x,x} \\right) & \\text{(2.5)}\n\\end{array}$
%[text] Then, the constitutive equations are given by the following relations:
%[text] $\\begin{array}{ll}\nq\_x = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_x & \\text{(3.1)} \\\\\nq\_y = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_y & \\text{(3.2)} \\\\\nm\_x = D \\left( \\kappa\_x + \\nu \\kappa\_y \\right) & \\text{(3.3)} \\\\\nm\_y = D \\left( \\kappa\_y + \\nu \\kappa\_x \\right) & \\text{(3.4)} \\\\\nm\_{xy} = D \\left( 1 - \\nu \\right) k\_{xy} , & \\text{(3.5)}\n\\end{array}$
%[text] Finally, the weak formulation of the problem writes as follows:
%[text] $\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\delta W\_{\\text{int}} + \\delta W\_{\\text{ext}} = 0, & \\text{(4.1)} \\\\\n-\\delta W\_{\\text{int}} = \\int\_{\\Omega} q\_x \\: \\delta \\gamma\_x + q\_y \\: \\delta \\gamma\_y + m\_x \\: \\delta \\kappa\_x + m\_y \\: \\delta \\kappa\_y + m\_{xy} \\: \\delta \\kappa\_{xy} \\: \\text{d} \\Omega , & \\text{(4.2)} \\\\\n\\delta W\_{\\text{ext}} = \\int\_{\\Omega} \\bar{p} \\delta w \\: \\text{d} \\Omega + \\int\_{\\subset\\partial \\Omega} \\bar{M}\_x \\: \\delta \\beta\_x \\: \\text{d} \\partial \\Omega + \\int\_{\\subset\\partial \\Omega} \\bar{M}\_y \\: \\delta \\beta\_y \\: \\text{d} \\partial \\Omega . & \\text{(4.3)}\n\\end{array}$
%[text] The problem under study herein is a cantilever plate subject to a distributed vertical load, see the following figure,
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![problemSetting_cantilever_plate.png](text:image:686e)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_C07E5A33] It is herein developed a Finite Element formulation for the Reissner-Mindlin plate theory and it is tested using both linear and bilinear basis functions. The solution of the problem setup depicted above along the $X$-direction is identical to the solution of the problem discussed in Live Script [`main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.m`](file:..\2_TimoshenkoBeam\main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.m), namely, the cantilever beam subject to a distributed vertical load. This solution is then invariant of the $Y$-location, since their is no variation of the solution along the $Y$-direction due to the given boundary conditions and geometry. Under these circumnstances the analytical solution of the problem can be "borrowed" by the equivalent Timoshenko cantilever beam which is presented [here](file:..\2_TimoshenkoBeam\main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.m), namely:
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_74A0D211] $w(X,Y) = \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A} \\left( \\overline{p} L X - \\frac{\\overline{p} X^2}{2} \\right) - \\frac{1}{EI} \\left( - \\frac{\\overline{p} X^4}{24} + \\frac{\\overline{p} L X^3}{6} - \\frac{\\overline{p} L^2 X^2}{4} \\right) \\qquad (5)$
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_EC97A06F] When substituting both the material and the kinematics relationships in the differential equation governing the Timoshenko beam problem, the final set of differential equations is the following one:
%[text] $\\overline{p} + \\alpha G A \\left( \\frac{\\partial^2 w}{\\partial X^2} + \\frac{\\partial \\beta\_y}{\\partial X} \\right) = 0 \\qquad (6.1) \\\\\n\\overline{m} + EI \\frac{\\partial^2 \\beta\_y}{\\partial X^2} = 0 \\qquad \\qquad \\qquad \\, (6.2) $
%[text] where the notations for the Reissner-Mindin plate have been kept. Moreover, the rotation around the $X$-axis, namely $\\beta\_x$, is equal to zero under the particular boundary and loading conditions. Similar to the derivation of equation (7) [here](file:../2_TimoshenkoBeam/main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.mlx:M_A96D07B3), substitung equation (1) into (2.1) and solving for $\\beta$ results in the following expression:
%[text] $\\beta\_y(X,Y) = \\int\_0^L - \\frac{1}{\\alpha G A} \\overline{p} - \\frac{\\partial^2 w}{\\partial X^2} \\; \\text{d} X \\qquad (7)$
%[text] The [Symbolic Math Toolbox](https://www.mathworks.com/products/symbolic.html)™ in [MATLAB](https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab.html)® can be used to obtain an analytical expression for equation (5) in only one line of code, see the corresponding [code section](internal:H_701C2D46).
%[text] 
%[text] [PREVIOUS Chapter](file:..\2_TimoshenkoBeam\main_Chapter2_ALinearStraightTimoshenkoBeamElement.m) *Finite Element Formulation of the linear Timoshenko beam*
